lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:7pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:7pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Income taxes (net of refunds)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1,748,024&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:1.5pt 0 1.5pt 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2,801,583&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:1.5pt 1pt 1.5pt 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;5. Related Party Transactions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Under a transmission service agreement with ComEd, the Company provides transmission service to ComEd over the Company's 345 kV transmission line, which interconnects the transmission systems of ComEd and Indiana Michigan Power Company, a subsidiary of American Electric Power Company. These services were recorded in Account 456, Other electric revenues, which is included within Operating Revenues on the Statement of Income and were $1,459,665 and $2,894,237 for the three months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively, and $3,844,718 and $5,781,987 for the six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The approximately 20 miles of transmission facilities owned by the Company are used as a &#8220;pass-through&#8221; for the purpose of wheeling electricity to and from the electricity grid of ComEd. ComEd&#8217;s transmission facilities and the Company&#8217;s transmission facilities are managed and operated as one integrated system under one transmission-pricing tariff. The Company and ComEd are integrated into PJM Interconnection, LLC (PJM). PJM allocates revenues and expenses to ComEd associated with the use of ComEd&#8217;s and the Company&#8217;s transmission facilities.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;ComEd provides labor, materials, and other services to the Company for the operation and maintenance of the transmission system, and Exelon Business Services Company (BSC) provides administrative services. These expenses are recorded in Accounts 568-574 and 923. ComEd provided labor, materials, and other services of $74,601 and $625,287 for the three months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively, and $152,375 and $1,083,493 for the six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively. In addition, no outside services were provided for the three and six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025. BSC provided no direct billed or administrative services for the three and six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:9pt;margin-top:9pt;text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As of June 30, 2026 and December 31, 2025, the Company had net payables of $361,574 and net receivables of $590,014, respectively, from ComEd, which are recorded in Account 146.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfApplicableNotesTextBlock>
